WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.980
"Frasier" was one of the most successful sitcoms&nbsp;
of the last 30 years, turning its talented cast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:09.420
into pop culture icons. Now Dr. Crane and&nbsp;
company are going back on the air. Here's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:13.888
what the cast has been up to since the original&nbsp;
series ended, and who's coming back for round two.

00:00:13.888 --> 00:00:18.060
The titular role of Dr. Frasier Crane, the&nbsp;
scholarly psychiatrist and host of "The Dr.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:22.620
Frasier Crane Show," is played by Kelsey Grammer.&nbsp;
His most iconic role to date, Grammer played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:28.140
Frasier for a total of 20 years including his time&nbsp;
on "Cheers." In a 2022 interview with Yahoo! News,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:31.378
when discussing his upcoming reprisal of&nbsp;
the role which made him a household name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.378 --> 00:00:35.241
Grammer described the character as, quote,&nbsp;
"like putting on a favorite old jacket."

00:00:35.241 --> 00:00:39.060
Since hanging up that uptight old jacket,&nbsp;
Grammer has worked prolifically in Hollywood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:43.800
as both actor and producer. Immediately following&nbsp;
the end of "Frasier," Grammer played Mr. Skibness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:48.360
in the Tenney Fairchild film, "The Good Humor&nbsp;
Man." In 2008, he starred as President Andrew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:51.840
Boone in the comedic political drama,&nbsp;
"Swing Vote," alongside Kevin Costner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:56.234
and he also appeared as himself in a handful&nbsp;
of episodes of the NBC comedy, "30 Rock."

00:00:56.234 --> 00:01:02.294
"One rule - anybody gets hurt during the&nbsp;
score, we leave them behind to die. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.682 --> 00:01:05.182
who are you and what are we doing?"

00:01:05.182 --> 00:01:09.300
Grammer would also dip his toe into the&nbsp;
blockbuster waters in 2006, starring as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:14.280
Beast in "X-Men: The Last Stand", returning to&nbsp;
the franchise in an uncredited cameo in "X-Men:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:19.620
Days of Future Past" and playing Harold Attinger&nbsp;
in "Transformers: Age of Extinction." Grammer also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:24.480
continues to provide the voice for Sideshow Bob in&nbsp;
"The Simpsons," which he first began in 1990. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:28.320
has several projects in production or development&nbsp;
besides the new "Frasier," with his latest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:32.640
project as of this video including a role in the&nbsp;
historical religious drama, "Jesus Revolution."
 &nbsp;

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:37.560
It might surprise fans of Martin Frasier's&nbsp;
live-in caretaker, Daphne Moon, to learn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:41.820
that British actress Jane Leeves almost didn't&nbsp;
land the role. According to Showbiz Cheat Sheet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:45.180
the production team had originally sought&nbsp;
out Rosie Perez for the role. Looking back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:50.580
on Leeves' 263 episodes now, it seems ridiculous&nbsp;
to think that anybody else was ever considered for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:54.480
the role of Daphne, a performance that&nbsp;
earned her an Emmy nomination in 1993.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:59.580
After "Frasier" came to an end in 2004, Leeves&nbsp;
took a two-year break from acting before returning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:03.780
to provide the voice of Eenie in "Garfield: A&nbsp;
Tale of Two Kitties." She worked regularly in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:07.560
television for several years, appearing in&nbsp;
shows like "Misconceptions" and "Desperate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:11.580
Housewives" and doing voiceover work for the&nbsp;
animated children's show, "Phineas and Ferb."

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:17.040
In 2010, Leeves landed the role of Joy Scroggs in&nbsp;
the TV Land sitcom, "Hot in Cleveland," which she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:20.880
played for five seasons. Leeves also starred&nbsp;
as Kit Voss in the Fox medical drama, "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:26.184
Resident," which concluded its sixth season in&nbsp;
January 2023 before being canceled months later.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:30.180
In 2021, Leeves told the Independent that&nbsp;
she wouldn't leave the show to join the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:33.840
Frasier revival, but now that the show is&nbsp;
over, maybe she found the time to join in.

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:39.360
Frasier's cold-hearted younger brother, Dr.&nbsp;
Niles Crane, is played by David Hyde Pierce.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:43.200
He won four Emmys for his performance,&nbsp;
only one fewer than Grammer, and was an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:46.800
integral part of the show's main cast.&nbsp;
Unfortunately for fans of Niles, Yahoo!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:51.000
News reported that the actor wasn't interested&nbsp;
in reprising his role in the upcoming reboot.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:55.800
After "Frasier," Hyde Pierce worked regularly in&nbsp;
theater. He played Sir Robin in the Monty Python&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.800 --> 00:03:01.080
musical, "Spamalot," from 2005-2006 and was&nbsp;
nominated for a Tony award for his performance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:06.420
as Horace Vandergelder in the 2017 revival of&nbsp;
"Hello, Dolly!" Hyde Pierce has also continued&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:10.560
to work in film and television. In 2010 he&nbsp;
starred in the comedic crime thriller, "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:16.260
Perfect Host," and in 2017, he reprised his role&nbsp;
as Professor Henry Neumann in the TV miniseries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:20.820
"Wet Hot American Summer: First Day of Camp."&nbsp;
He also played Frank Prady in Season 6 of "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:25.200
Good Wife," and lent his voice to an episode&nbsp;
of "Sesame Street," as well as voicing Cecil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:28.500
the brother of Grammer's Sideshow Bob&nbsp;
in three episodes of "The Simpsons."

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:31.560
"But you'll find one gets more&nbsp;
respect as a humble civil servant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:34.500
than as a homicidal maniac&nbsp;
- or as a clown's sidekick."

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:38.940
Hyde Pierce returned to television in&nbsp;
2022 to play Julia Child's wife, Paul,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:44.100
in the HBO Max series, "Julia." He is also set to&nbsp;
star as Father Conor in the upcoming horror film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:47.820
"The Georgetown Project" which is&nbsp;
slated for release sometime in 2023.

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:52.980
The producer of Frasier's radio show, Roz&nbsp;
Doyle, is played by Peri Gilpin. Encouraged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:56.580
by her parents to pursue her passion for acting&nbsp;
since she was a child growing up in Dallas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.580 --> 00:04:00.420
Gilpin studied acting at the University&nbsp;
of Texas and the British American Dramatic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:04.140
Academy in London. According to Ability&nbsp;
Magazine, she performed on stage in New&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:07.560
York before relocating to Los Angeles&nbsp;
and landing her big break on "Frasier."

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:12.180
Gilpin is still working regularly on television.&nbsp;
Following the end of "Frasier," Gilpin popped up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:16.560
in shows like "Medium," "Desperate Housewives,"&nbsp;
and Law &amp; Order: Criminal Intent," and lent her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:21.300
vocal talents to the animated comedy, "King&nbsp;
of the Hill." In 2011, Gilpin had an on-screen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:25.800
reunion with her "Frasier" co-star Jane Leeves&nbsp;
in an episode of "Hot in Cleveland." Arguably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:30.240
her biggest TV role since "Frasier" was as&nbsp;
Kim Keeler in the ABC sports drama, "Make It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:35.400
or Break It," which ran for four seasons. Gilpin&nbsp;
also appeared in a 2017 episode of "Broad City."

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:38.940
In later years, Gilpin has been working&nbsp;
more on the big screen with smaller roles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.940 --> 00:04:43.080
in movies like "Occupy, Texas," "Flock&nbsp;
of Dudes," and "The Outdoorsman." Recent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:47.340
roles include the romantic comedy, "We Broke&nbsp;
Up," playing Mrs. Donahue in "Rushed," and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:51.540
Sara Hughes in the historical drama, "The&nbsp;
Anthrax Attacks." According to Deadline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:54.600
she's back on board for the Frasier&nbsp;
revival in a recurring role.

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:57.360
The role of Martin, the Crane&nbsp;
brothers' retired-cop father,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:01.440
was played by the late John Mahoney. For his&nbsp;
performance, Mahoney was nominated for two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:07.380
Primetime Emmys in 1999 and 2003. Mahoney's&nbsp;
long career began in 1981 and spanned nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:12.600
four decades before he passed away in 2018&nbsp;
at 77. Despite his success in Hollywood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:15.720
he seemed to prefer living in&nbsp;
Chicago and performing on the stage.

00:05:15.935 --> 00:05:21.575
"I just want to be back home, and I want&nbsp;
to be on stage and um, where I enjoy it."

00:05:21.575 --> 00:05:25.080
After his role in "Frasier," Mahoney&nbsp;
provided the voice of Papi in "Kronk's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:29.460
New Groove" and appeared in a&nbsp;
2006 episode of "ER." In 2007,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:33.120
he landed a major film role as Poppy&nbsp;
in the Steve Carell romantic comedy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:37.920
"Dan in Real Life," and lent his voice to a Season&nbsp;
19 episode of "The Simpsons." The following year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:42.180
Mahoney appeared in the first season of "In&nbsp;
Treatment" and featured in two episodes of the USA&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:47.220
action drama, "Burn Notice." In 2011, he joined&nbsp;
the cast of the "Frasier" cast magnet, "Hot in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:52.380
Cleveland," and his final performance before his&nbsp;
passing was in a 2015 episode of "Foyle's War."

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:57.540
Martin's loveable canine companion Eddie was&nbsp;
played by an adorable four-legged thespian by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.540 --> 00:06:01.080
the name of Moose. He's appeared in most&nbsp;
episodes besides the five members of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:06.840
main cast — a total of 163 episodes across ten&nbsp;
seasons. Frasier is often at odds with Eddie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.840 --> 00:06:10.800
taking umbrage with his propensity to stare&nbsp;
fixedly at him for prolonged periods of time.

00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:19.306
"I said down. Good boy Eddie, just get down.&nbsp;
Goo-googood Eddie. Get down - GET DOWN!"

00:06:19.306 --> 00:06:23.760
During his distinguished 10-year career, this&nbsp;
diminutive Jack Russel terrier logged four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:28.380
acting credits. "Frasier" was his small screen&nbsp;
debut, but after his first season on the air,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.380 --> 00:06:33.540
he immediately booked a job in an episode of "The&nbsp;
5 Mrs. Buchanans" as Petey. The following year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:37.140
he played Monte in an episode of&nbsp;
the comedy, "High Society." In 2000,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:40.920
Moose made the jump to Hollywood, starring&nbsp;
as the titular dog in the film. "My Dog&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:45.750
Skip," which also featured a performance by&nbsp;
his son, Enzo. "Frasier" was Moose's last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:51.060
onscreen performance before he passed away in&nbsp;
2006 at the ripe old age of 112 in dog years.

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:57.360
Frasier isn't the only colorful personality&nbsp;
at Seattle's KACL radio station. Following the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:02.160
psychiatrist's time slot is the "Gonzo Sports&nbsp;
Show," and its host, the macho, womanizing Bob&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:07.260
"Bulldog" Briscoe, played by Dan Butler. Butler&nbsp;
popped up in 53 episodes of "Frasier" beginning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:11.504
in its first season, and it wasn't immediately&nbsp;
clear if he's coming back for the revival.

00:07:11.504 --> 00:07:15.540
After his final appearance in 2004, Butler&nbsp;
stayed active in the television industry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.540 --> 00:07:18.960
continuing to lend his voice to "Hey&nbsp;
Arnold!" on Nickelodeon as several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:22.680
characters including Mr. Simmons, and&nbsp;
appearing in episodes of "Quintuplets"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:26.220
and "Dr. Vegas." He's popped up on&nbsp;
several popular shows since then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.220 --> 00:07:30.360
including one-episode roles in "Malcolm in the&nbsp;
Middle," "Supernatural," "House," and "Monk."

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:35.220
In 2011, he returned to the big screen, appearing&nbsp;
as Steve Carell's boss in the romantic comedy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:39.900
"Crazy, Stupid Love." In 2020, Butler played&nbsp;
Dr. Alan Mendelson in the romantic drama,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:45.120
"All My Life," and in 2022, he was featured&nbsp;
in the Marilyn Monroe biopic, "Blonde."

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:49.920
Another KACL personality, restaurant critic Gil&nbsp;
Chesterton is a recurring character throughout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.920 --> 00:07:54.960
the show's 11-season run. He is played by Royal&nbsp;
Academy of Dramatic Art alum Edward Hibbert. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:59.880
appeared in a total of 29 episodes, including&nbsp;
an appearance in the series finale in 2004.

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:03.420
Hibbert has worked steadily since the end&nbsp;
of "Frasier." After the series concluded,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.420 --> 00:08:06.180
he played Sir Michael Strickland in&nbsp;
the romantic thriller, "A Different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:09.720
Loyalty." In 2006, he was featured&nbsp;
in the Christopher Nolan thriller,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:13.834
"The Prestige" and appeared as Randall&nbsp;
Farber in an episode of "Gilmore Girls."

00:08:13.834 --> 00:08:17.940
Much like several of his "Frasier" co-stars, he&nbsp;
also appeared in an episode of "Hot in Cleveland"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:23.400
in 2014. In 2018, Hibbert also provided the&nbsp;
voice of a talking parrot umbrella in "Mary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:29.040
Poppins Returns" and was featured in episodes of&nbsp;
"Grace and Frankie" and "Blue Bloods" in 2019.

00:08:29.040 --> 00:08:34.140
KACL's technical assistant Noel Shempsky, is&nbsp;
played by television journeyman Patrick Kerr.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.282 --> 00:08:39.262
First introduced in Season 1, Kerr would star&nbsp;
in a total of 22 episodes over the 11 seasons.

00:08:39.262 --> 00:08:45.060
"I have a secret weapon. I can&nbsp;
faint at will. Check it out."

00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:52.140
After "Frasier," Kerr appeared in an episode of&nbsp;
the much less successful sitcom spinoff series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.140 --> 00:08:56.100
"Joey." He followed that up with a&nbsp;
small role in the 2005 action biopic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.100 --> 00:09:00.540
"Domino" as a DMV manager, and the following&nbsp;
year appeared in an episode of the CBS sitcom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:04.500
"The New Adventures of Old Christine." Over&nbsp;
the next decade, he had several one-episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:08.805
roles in shows such as "CSI," "Jessie,"&nbsp;
"Elementary," and "Hannah Montana."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.805 --> 00:09:14.685
Later film roles include the 2014 comedy,&nbsp;
"Friends and Romans" and 2015's "Anesthesia."

00:09:14.685 --> 00:09:17.400
Since then, Kerr has worked in&nbsp;
short films and on episodes of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:22.800
"Little America" on Apple TV+ and the HBO&nbsp;
Max crime comedy, "Search Party." In 2023,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:27.060
he appeared as a main cast member in another&nbsp;
Apple TV+ original, "The Big Door Prize."

00:09:27.060 --> 00:09:31.800
First appearing in Season 5, KACL's&nbsp;
station manager Kenny Daly is played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:36.120
by Tom McGowan. He worked primarily in film&nbsp;
before appearing in "Frasier," beginning in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:40.560
1992 with a role in Michael Mann's historical&nbsp;
drama, "The Last of the Mohicans" and had some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:44.063
experience in the sitcom world, playing&nbsp;
Bernie in "Everybody Loves Raymond."

00:09:44.063 --> 00:09:48.960
Following "Frasier," McGowan stayed busy. In&nbsp;
2004, he had a lead role in the romantic comedy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:52.980
"Dog Gone Love." Since then, he's stayed in the&nbsp;
world of television, featuring in shows such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.980 --> 00:09:58.620
as "Hannah Montana," "Boston Legal," and "CSI."&nbsp;
Unsurprisingly, he also wound up in a 2011 episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.620 --> 00:10:02.159
of "Hot in Cleveland" and the following year&nbsp;
he showed up in an episode of "Modern Family."

00:10:02.159 --> 00:10:07.380
In 2015, McGowan played Congressman Moyes&nbsp;
in an episode of the HBO political comedy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.380 --> 00:10:09.660
"Veep," and returned to the&nbsp;
big screen to play a role in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:13.020
"Freeheld" alongside Julianne Moore&nbsp;
and Michael Shannon. Two years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.020 --> 00:10:17.340
he had a supporting role as Jax Rindell in&nbsp;
Season 1 of "The Good Fight." His most recent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.340 --> 00:10:22.164
role was in 2022 when McGowan appeared in an&nbsp;
episode of the NBC comedy, "American Auto."

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:26.340
Daphne Moon's overbearing mother,&nbsp;
Gertrude, who first appears in Season 7,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.340 --> 00:10:30.480
is portrayed by veteran actress Millicent&nbsp;
Martin. Martin's career began in British&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:34.260
television all the way back in 1957 and&nbsp;
she would make appearances throughout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.260 --> 00:10:37.320
the remainder of the show, with her&nbsp;
final appearance coming in Season 11.

00:10:37.320 --> 00:10:41.880
After her final appearance in "Frasier," Martin&nbsp;
popped up in a 2004 episode of "The Drew Carey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.880 --> 00:10:45.300
Show" and appeared in "Will &amp; Grace" the&nbsp;
following year. Her television career has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:49.680
been going strong since then, and she has acted&nbsp;
in episodes of "Chuck," "Castle," "Bones," and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:54.180
"Modern Family." Not wanting to miss out on the&nbsp;
"Frasier" cast's favorite studio lot hangout spot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.180 --> 00:10:57.900
she appeared in two episodes of "Hot in&nbsp;
Cleveland," and appeared in a small role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.900 --> 00:11:03.420
in the 2017 movie, "The Last Word." In 2017,&nbsp;
McGowan took on the role of Joan-Margaret in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.420 --> 00:11:07.140
the Netflix original comedy series, "Grace&nbsp;
and Frankie," first appearing in Season 3&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.140 --> 00:11:12.043
and playing the character in 20 episodes to&nbsp;
the show's seventh and final season in 2022.

